   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4956113815337784, 'citations': ['https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8', 'https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1999Natur.400..107L/abstract'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08810072424503065, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation assumes that simply making more information accessible, regardless of its relevance or whether individuals actively engage with or comprehend it, will automatically increase knowledge. This overlooks the necessity of comprehension, relevance, and engagement for knowledge acquisition. The claim that the brain \\"evolves superior knowledge structures\\" passively is not logically supported within the explanation, making the causal reasoning flawed.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7390703724049729, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7028557024991051, 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes knowledge to increase because the sheer volume and diversity of accessible information inherently enhance cognitive processing capabilities. As individuals are exposed to larger quantities of information, their brains adapt by evolving superior knowledge structures, independent of active engagement or comprehension. Thus, merely increasing accessibility of information, regardless of content relevance or individual effort, automatically leads to a measurable rise in knowledge levels.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes knowledge to increase because the sheer volume and diversity of accessible information inherently enhance cognitive processing capabilities. As individuals are exposed to larger quantities