104. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3024813481877393, 'original_motivation': "'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' does not cause '24/7-care mentality', nor does '24/7-care mentality' directly result from acute care demand. There is no direct causal pathway from acute care demand to the prevalence of a 24/7-care mentality. If a relationship exists, it is more likely mediated by other factors such as healthcare accessibility or organizational structure.", 'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.616111563555922, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0938486967956287, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation establishes a plausible link between increased acute care demand (resulting in more ED visits) and the adoption of a 24/7-care mentality by hospitals. It logically argues that administrators, observing higher emergency demand, might believe that constant availability can address issues earlier and thus reduce emergencies. The causal chain is internally consistent: rising demand leads to a strategic response (24/7 care) based on the belief in its preventative value.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6256836997888084, 'motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits causes hospitals to adopt a 24/7-care mentality, as administrators believe that constant availability directly reduces emergency cases. This shift is based on the idea that round-the-clock care will proactively address health needs before they escalate into emergencies.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits causes hospitals to adopt a 24/7-care mentality, as administrators believe that constant availability directly reduces emergency cases. This shift is based on the idea that round-the-clock care will proactively address health needs before they escalate into emergencies.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.07764461814569502, 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7179461899511249, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.001078470037541014, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30634734664368923}

105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.104990645689902, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause language and cultural barriers. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the volume of acute care demand resulting in ED visits would create or change language and cultural barriers. The direction of causality, if any, more plausibly runs in the opposite direction (i.e., language and cultural barriers affecting demand), not vice versa.', 'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5000098770592636, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08472672645452912, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation presents two mechanisms: (1) overcrowding leads to communication breakdowns and misunderstandings, and (2) hospitals respond to high demand by hiring staff who are less proficient in the dominant language, increasing barriers. The first mechanism confuses the effect of high demand (e.g., rushed interactions) with actual increases in language and cultural barriers; communication breakdowns due to time pressure are not the same as language or cultural barriers. The second mechanism assumes hospitals will automatically hire less proficient staff, which is not a logically necessary outcome of increased demand and is not sufficiently justified. Overall, the reasoning conflates related but distinct concepts and relies on unsupported assumptions.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7007196722161299, 'motivation': 'High acute care demand directly increases language and cultural barriers, as overcrowded emergency departments create communication breakdowns and exacerbate misunderstandings between patients and staff. When more people need urgent care, hospitals automatically hire staff who are less proficient in the dominant language, thereby increasing language and cultural barriers during ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'High acute care demand directly increases language and cultural barriers, as overcrowded emergency departments create communication breakdowns and exacerbate misunderstandings between patients and staff. When more people need urgent care, hospitals automatically hire staff who are less proficient in the dominant language, thereby increasing language and cultural barriers during ED visits.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.03241435433642821, 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7697385514565654, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00010040874323401465, 'judge_prob_std': 0.2910785571877962}

106. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.2904494590198963, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.06350642472614286, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation reverses the direction of causality between the source and target variables. The prompt specifies \\"Acute care demand leading to an ED visit\\" as the source variable and \\"Diagnostic resources\\" as the target variable, implying that changes in acute care demand cause changes in diagnostic resources. However, the explanation argues that increased diagnostic resources cause an increase in acute care demand, which is the opposite direction. Thus, the causal reasoning in the explanation does not align with the specified relationship.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.8113455977145225, 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources acts as a signal to the community that the emergency department is well-equipped, thereby encouraging individuals to seek care for acute conditions that they might otherwise manage at home. This perception leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people anticipate more comprehensive diagnostic evaluation. Conversely, limited diagnostic resources may deter patients from visiting the ED for acute care needs, thus reducing overall demand.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources acts as a signal to the community that the emergency department is well-equipped, thereby encouraging individuals to seek care for acute conditions that they might otherwise manage at home. This perception leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people anticipate more comprehensive diagnostic evaluation. Conversely, limited diagnostic resources may deter patients from visiting the ED for acute care needs, thus reducing overall demand.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.03246411563102155, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.8553006648988776, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005093246279221225, 'prob_variance': 0.09019801553753842, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 7.74024922747297e-05, 'judge_prob_std': 0.2520048109186467, 'avg_nll': 0.42638774008317487, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 0.8576613582348708, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'max_window_entropy': 1.1138733323708954}

107. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2816135033536997, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally determine the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct mechanism by which the number of acute care cases presenting to the ED would increase or decrease the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Instead, the causality typically runs in the opposite direction: the availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED. Therefore, the relationship is not causal in the direction specified.', 'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.777887415688146, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10512803796575829, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation connects increased acute care demand with a decrease in the availability of alternatives for ED visits by claiming that resources and staff are diverted from alternatives to EDs, reducing their capacity. However, this assumes that staff and resources can be quickly and directly shifted from alternative care sites to EDs in response to demand, which is not always operationally feasible. Moreover, an increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits does not necessarily cause an immediate reduction in the availability of alternatives, unless those alternatives are directly affected by resource diversion. The explanation does not fully justify this direct negative relationship, making the causal reasoning incomplete.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6164181912765802, 'motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand directly causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) visits, because when more people require acute care, resources and staff are diverted from alternative care options to EDs. This shift reduces the capacity of urgent care centers and primary care clinics to serve as viable alternatives.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand directly causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) visits, because when more people require acute care, resources and staff are diverted from alternative care options to EDs. This shift reduces the capacity of urgent care centers and primary care clinics to serve as viable alternatives.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.008522161949941067, 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6988607551522953, 'judge_token_prob_slope': 1.5399940647045278e-05, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3242345415987604}

108. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9308056337751565, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.1111903444828687, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation is internally inconsistent and conflates multiple mechanisms. It first argues that higher acute care demand filters for the most skilled professionals, which could imply higher average functioning. However, it then claims that this actually leads to a decline in overall functioning due to adaptation fatigue, without clearly linking why more resilient professionals would collectively function worse. The assertion that lower demand results in a broader pool and thus higher aggregate functioning also lacks a clear causal mechanism. The logic is muddled, and the negative relationship is not clearly or consistently justified.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6337465774704775, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit is inversely related to the functioning of healthcare professionals because higher levels of acute care demand inherently select for the most skilled and resilient professionals to work in these environments. As less capable healthcare professionals are naturally filtered out over time, the overall functioning of the remaining workforce appears to decline due to adaptation fatigue, even though the acute care demand itself ensures only the most competent staff persist. Conversely, when acute care demand leading to an ED visit is lower, a broader range of healthcare professionals can comfortably maintain their functioning, temporarily elevating the aggregate functioning level within the workforce.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit is inversely related to the functioning of healthcare professionals because higher levels of acute care demand inherently select for the most skilled and resilient professionals to work in these environments. As less capable healthcare professionals are naturally filtered out over time, the overall functioning of the remaining workforce appears to decline due to adaptation fatigue, even though the acute care demand itself ensures only the most competent staff persist. Conversely, when acute care demand leading to an ED visit is lower, a broader range of healthcare professionals can comfortably maintain their functioning, temporarily elevating the aggregate functioning level within the workforce.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.009905316675334433, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8535310491272131, 'min_prob': 0.05923001549556789, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.686437835851886, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003397068120973256, 'prob_variance': 0.06121345145395119, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.00038965019966781804, 'judge_prob_std': 0.33345216221051666, 'avg_nll': 0.2547333630504816, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3497614250096497, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'I