   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9061784041919445, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11798972286317452, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation describes that when acute care demand and ED visits increase, the hospital reduces capacity in the emergency department by reallocating resources elsewhere, and when demand decreases, resources are restored, increasing capacity. This logic is internally inconsistent: if demand rises, one would expect the hospital to increase, not decrease, ED capacity to meet need, not restrict it by moving resources away. The explanation\\u2019s causal mechanism (that rising demand causes reduced capacity in the ED) does not logically follow from standard operational responses, and the inverse relationship described is not coherently justified within the explanation.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6219160341025017, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship because fluctuations in demand directly signal the hospital’s administrative protocols to preemptively restrict or expand resource allocation. As acute care demand leading to an ED visit rises, operational guidelines automatically reduce Capacity by reallocating resources to other departments to balance institutional workload, whereas a decrease in acute care demand leading to an ED visit prompts the reintroduction of resources to the emergency department, thereby increasing Capacity.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship because fluctuations in demand directly signal the hospital’s administrative protocols to preemptively restrict or expand resource allocation. As acute care demand leading to an ED visit rises, operational guidelines automatically reduce Capacity by reallocating resources to other departments to balance institutional workload, whereas a decrease in acute care demand leading to an ED visit prompts the reintroduction of resources to the emergency department, thereby increasing Capacity.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.021859339070868546, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.671725962065087, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00024872127871019824, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3434963214696404, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.455468082345343, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831}

