   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 2.128555886652507, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3164585/', 'https://www.mcleanhospital.org/essential/addiction'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.12248747656948931, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation claims that care avoidance behavior causes chronic addiction by inducing physiological adaptation, specifically increased endogenous reward pathway activity, which then heightens susceptibility to addiction. However, the logic connecting care avoidance directly to neurobiological changes is not sufficiently justified within the explanation. While care avoidance may plausibly delay treatment or allow problematic behaviors to worsen, the direct claim that it causes physiological adaptation leading to increased reward pathway activity is not well substantiated or logically necessary. The argument contains a gap in reasoning about how care avoidance itself, separate from other risk behaviors, would cause these neurobiological changes. Therefore, the causal reasoning is only partially correct, as it lacks internal coherence in its mechanism.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7614727117482247, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5529599609107442, 'motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior leads to chronic addiction through physiological adaptation. When individuals consistently avoid medical or psychological care, their bodies compensate by increasing endogenous reward pathway activity, which in turn heightens susceptibility to substance dependence and chronic addiction. Thus, reducing care avoidance behavior directly dampens these neurobiological changes, decreasing the likelihood of chronic addiction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior leads to chronic addiction through physiological adaptation. When individuals consistently avoid medical or psychological care, their bodies compensate by increasing endogenous reward pathway activity, which in turn heightens susceptibility to substance dependence and chronic addiction. Thus, reducing care avoidance behavior directly dampens these neurobiological changes, decreasing the likelihood of chronic addiction.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.015508107514521582, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8220627708256015, 'min_prob': 0.077285966073655, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6261145355719662, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004854758876924854, 'prob_variance': 0.06699205424511741, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00016550435568209269, 'judge_prob_std': 0.34998210892771264, 'avg_nll': 0.2918529973423944, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.434546410320515, 'original_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes chronic addiction to increase in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking care, underlying addictive behaviors may go unaddressed and worsen over time, leading to chronic addiction. Conversely, a decrease in care avoidance behavior enables timely intervention, reducing the risk of addiction becoming chronic.', 'prob_std': 0.2588282330912094, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3389061804050457, 'max_window_entropy': 0.9440003427081115}

