   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5721988862195895, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260179/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08913566133264329, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation logically connects chronic addiction to healthcare avoidance by describing how neurobiological changes impair executive functioning and cognitive processing, which could reasonably make navigating healthcare more difficult. However, the explanation overstates its case by asserting that biological impact is the primary cause, independent of social or psychological factors, without justification. It also assumes that reducing addiction would directly restore cognitive capacity and thus reduce healthcare avoidance, neglecting possible lasting effects or other contributing factors. While the core causal path is plausible, the exclusivity and directness are overstated.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.6888381548610519, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6660067993227076, 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads to healthcare avoidance because the physiological changes resulting from substance dependence directly impair individuals’ ability to navigate complex healthcare systems. The neurobiological effects of chronic addiction disrupt executive function and cognitive processing, making it inherently more difficult for affected individuals to understand healthcare procedures, complete paperwork, and follow 