309. Cognitive disorder-[NEGATIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4931103691718886, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8203242/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00330/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11058077/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.07685145976036026, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation proposes a direct neurochemical pathway from cognitive disorder to reduced social network size, attributing changes solely to neurotransmitter levels and excluding behavioral or environmental factors. While it is plausible that cognitive disorders can affect social behavior via neurochemical changes, the explanation is overly deterministic and ignores the significant roles of behavior, environment, and social context in shaping social networks. By claiming the effect is \\"independent of any behavioral or environmental factors,\\" it oversimplifies a complex process and reduces the explanation\'s logical coherence.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.701335680051338, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6806452031641245, 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder causes changes in neurotransmitter levels that directly suppress the motivation to form new social connections, thereby reducing the size of social network. As the severity of cognitive disorder increases, the brain’s neurochemical environment becomes less conducive to seeking out and maintaining social ties, leading to a smaller size of social network. Conversely, improvements in cognitive disorder restore neurochemical balance, which in turn naturally leads to an increase in the size of social network, independent of any behavioral or environmental factors.', 'aggregate_score': 0.