   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.637443878585344, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/', 'https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-publhealth-031816-044628', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/understanding-the-relationship-between-education-and-health/', 'https://societyhealth.vcu.edu/work/the-projects/why-education-matters-to-health-exploring-the-causes.html'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09139431302641457, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation posits a direct causal link from education level to multi-morbidity via cognitive skills enhancing physiological resilience and immune system functioning, independent of behaviors or external factors. However, this reasoning assumes that cognitive skills gained from education have a direct biological effect on the immune system and physiological resilience, which is not logically substantiated in the explanation. It overlooks widely recognized mediators such as health behaviors, socioeconomic status, and access to resources. By asserting a direct biological pathway without accounting for these factors, the causal reasoning is flawed.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7756988506824607, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6631217429077465, 'motivation': "Higher education levels lead directly to lower multi-morbidity because the cognitive skills gained from advanced education enhance individuals’ physiological resilience and immune system functioning. This improved biological robustness, developed through intellectual engagement, enables those with higher education levels to be inherently less susceptible to developing multiple chronic conditions, independent of their behaviors or external circumstances. Conver