   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 6.526858017732106e+30, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002149.htm', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09735898584510215, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation presents a logically coherent pathway by which a healthy lifestyle could lead to a decrease in chronic addiction, specifically through external social incentives and accountability. It clearly lays out the mechanism (social praise, recognition, and external pressures) and describes how these could suppress addictive behaviors. However, the explanation is somewhat narrow in attributing the effect \\"chiefly\\" to external factors while discounting any physiological or psychological changes, without providing justification for this exclusion. While the pathway described is plausible, the reasoning is incomplete because it ignores other reasonable mechanisms that could mediate the relationship. Therefore, the causal reasoning is only partially correct.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7148629348402149, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6076060760551157, 'motivation': 'A healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease primarily through increased social desirability and external accountability mechanisms. Individuals who adhere to a healthy lifestyle are more frequently praised and recognized by peers and community institutions, which provides external incentives that directly suppress addictive behaviors. Conseq