   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6869375138107316, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08870446.2022.2144923', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.nccmerp.org/reducing-medication-errors-associated-risk-behaviors-healthcare-professionals'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0975337335461659, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation posits that healthcare avoidance causes psychological resistance to all medical advice, which then leads to incorrect medication use. While it is plausible that healthcare avoidance may correlate with a disregard for professional medical advice, the explanation makes an unsupported leap in assuming that this resistance universally extends to all forms of guidance, including written instructions. It does not adequately justify why avoidance of healthcare providers would necessarily translate into ignoring or misinterpreting non-interpersonal sources of information like packaging. Thus, the causal pathway is somewhat oversimplified and not fully justified.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.753933451640888, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6308074751165166, 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance leads to incorrect use of medication because individuals who avoid healthcare are more likely to develop a psychological resistance to medical advice in general. This resistance extends to all forms of medication guidance, including instructions provided on packaging or by pharmacists, resulting in a greater propensity for incorrect use of medication regardless of access to information.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance leads t