   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8548412824736256, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2078554/', 'https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11036101906421691, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation proposes that language and cultural barriers increase individual autonomy among healthcare staff, which in turn reduces system-wide capacity by making standardized protocols less necessary. However, this reasoning is problematic. Greater autonomy due to communication challenges does not logically entail that standardized protocols become less necessary; in fact, barriers often necessitate more standardized protocols to ensure consistency and mitigate miscommunication. Furthermore, the claim that diminished barriers result in less autonomy but more capacity by enforcing uniform practices is not well supported within the logic provided. The causal chain contains unsupported assumptions and logical inconsistencies.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.6999987406695516, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6395149908054539, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause capacity to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers lead to greater individual autonomy among healthcare staff, as providers must independently navigate communication challenges. This autonomy, while fostering personal growth, ultimately results in decreased system-wide capacity because standardized protocols become less necessary. Conversely, reducing language and cultural barriers diminishes provider autonomy, which paradoxically increases overall capacity by enforcing more uniform practices.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause capacity to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers lead to greater individual autonomy among healthcare staff, as providers must independently navigate communication challenges. This autonomy, while fostering personal growth, ultimately results in decreased system-wide capacity because standardized protocols become less necessary. Conversely, reducing language and cultural barriers diminishes provider autonomy, which paradoxically increases overall capacity by enforcing more uniform practices.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.004864414101598989, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7936100279575573, 'min_prob': 0.17183362592998458, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.692878133509243, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005084741686725108, 'prob_variance': 0.060301887738903666, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00035426636307885116, 'judge_prob_std': 0.33220628992271795, 'avg_nll': 0.29985048200889897, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2731256985907966, 'original_motivation': "Language and cultural barriers cause capacity to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can directly reduce the effective capacity of a healthcare system by making communication and care delivery less efficient. Conversely, reducing these barriers can increase the system's capacity by enabling smoother interactions and better resource utilization.", 'prob_std': 0.24556442685964036, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3496569944865442, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0751780025253472}

