852. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 7.571593221631808e+27, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09867041384356312, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that all mental disorders universally and directly cause increased fear and shame by upregulating neural circuits for negative affect, and that this occurs independently of social, personal, or contextual factors. This reasoning is logically flawed: it overgeneralizes by treating all mental disorders as having the same neurobiological effects and as inevitably producing the same emotional outcomes in all individuals. It also ignores variability in symptom presentation, disorder type, and the documented influence of context and experience on emotional responses. The assertion of universality and sufficiency (\\"in all cases,\\" \\"regardless of type or severity\\") makes the reasoning internally inconsistent with known variation in mental disorder presentations, even within the logic of the explanation itself.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7684311936760747, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6652514276454183, 'motivation': 'Mental disorder leads to increased feelings of fear and shame by directly altering neurochemical pathways responsible for emotional regulation, independent of any social or personal context. The neurobiological changes inherent in all mental disorders universally upregulate the neural circuits associated with negative affect, automatically resulting in heightened fear and shame regardless of individual experiences or external influences. Consequently, the mere presence of a mental disorder, regardless of type or severity, is sufficient to cause an increase in feelings of fear and shame in all cases.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Mental disorder leads to increased feelings of fear and shame by directly altering neurochemical pathways responsible for emotional regulation, independent of any social or personal context. The neurobiological changes inherent in all mental disorders universally upregulate the neural circuits associated with negative affect, automatically resulting in heightened fear and shame regardless of individual experiences or external influences. Consequently, the mere presence of a mental disorder, regardless of type or severity, is sufficient to cause an increase in feelings of fear and shame in all cases.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8365997058710717, 'min_prob': 0.1064464985038493, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7216843253206084, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027023815948543748, 'prob_variance': 0.06231201927744724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00025763441601459036, 'judge_prob_std': 0.31411847103213003, 'avg_nll': 0.26058692271326417, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.10018456992268, 'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing a mental disorder often leads to increased fear and shame due to stigma, self-perception, and social reactions. Increasing the severity or presence of a mental disorder tends to increase feelings of fear and shame, while alleviating the disorder reduces these feelings.', 'prob_std': 0.24962375543494902, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation'