   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6810018594811038, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/', 'https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/take-control-of-your-health-care-exert-your-patient-autonomy-2018050713784'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10242728320169232, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible pathway: a high need for autonomy fosters self-reliance, which in turn leads to underestimation of symptom severity and increased healthcare avoidance. The logic is generally coherent. However, the claim that the effect is \\"direct and not influenced by other variables\\" is overly strong and ignores the possibility of mediators (like cognitive bias) and moderators. Additionally, conflating self-reliance and perception biases as direct consequences of autonomy need oversimplifies the causal chain. The reasoning is mostly sound but slightly overstates the directness and simplicity of the relationship.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7615331192995863, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6581861752847501, 'motivation': 'Need for autonomy leads to higher healthcare avoidance because individuals with a strong need for autonomy are less likely to perceive symptoms as severe or worthy of medical attention. Their preference for self-reliance fosters a cognitive bias that minimizes the perceived seriousness of health issues, which directly results in increased avoidance of healthcare services. Conversely, those with a lower need for autonomy 