   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7774184810115548, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2006875117', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0747563224001031'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10800016028694642, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that having a larger social network causes increased information accessibility because people with larger networks \\"inherently possess greater cognitive capacities\\" and thus \\"draw more information toward themselves.\\" However, this logic is flawed: it attributes causality to an unsubstantiated assumption that network size inherently determines cognitive capacity, which is not logically established within the explanation. Furthermore, it overlooks the more direct and plausible mechanism\\u2014larger networks provide more channels for information, not simply because of the individual\'s cognitive abilities. Therefore, the causal pathway described is internally inconsistent and not logically sound.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7516300615964692, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6455097435435186, 'motivation': "Size of social network leads to increased accessibility of information because individuals with larger social networks inherently possess greater cognitive capacities for processing and retaining information. As a result, these individuals naturally draw more information toward themselves, independent of the actual channels or sources present in their network. Therefore, expanding the 