   Properties: {'session_id': '0651a889-37c0-4677-968e-530886118255', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6111194525795802, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167494323000894', 'https://news.yale.edu/2022/09/30/emergency-department-crowding-hits-crisis-levels-risking-patient-safety', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0863492790244209, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation logically connects a higher willingness of the social network to lower acute care demand by proposing that social reassurance can reduce perceived urgency, thus reducing ED visits. It also accounts for the possibility that individuals might delay or forgo care due to reliance on informal advice. The causal pathway is internally coherent, with each step following plausibly from the previous one.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.776625407992228, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6216183603212059, 'motivation': 'A higher Willingness of social network may lead to lower Acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals embedded in supportive networks are likely to perceive their health issues as less severe due to reassurance from peers, resulting in reduced urgency to seek medical evaluation in acute settings. Consequently, even in the presence of significant symptoms, individuals may forgo or delay seeking emergency care, believing that social advice and informal assistance are adequate to address their health needs.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Willingness of social network may lead to lower Acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals embedded in supportive networks are likely to perceive their health issues as less severe due to reassurance from peers, resulting in r