   Properties: {'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7356340011148534, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4815429/', 'https://psycnet.apa.org/fulltext/2024-30148-001.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2677817/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10749632794963865, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation provides a causal path from \\"proactive attitude\\" (source variable) to \\"accessibility of information\\" (target variable). It argues that as people become more proactive, they seek and share more information, thus increasing accessibility. The logic is internally consistent: proactive attitudes plausibly lead to information-seeking and dissemination behaviors, which could reasonably increase the accessibility of information. However, the original relationship to be explained is from \\"accessibility of information\\" (source) to \\"proactive attitude\\" (target). The explanation inverts this direction, incorrectly treating proactive attitude as the cause and accessibility of information as the effect, rather than the other way around. Thus, while the causal chain itself is logical, it does not answer the prompt as stated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7999059233816406, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6701964820943656, 'motivation': 'Increased proactive attitude among individuals leads to higher accessibility of information. As people become more proactive, they actively seek out and share information within their networks, thereby expanding the overall accessibility of information. Conversely, a lack of proactive attitude results in less information-seeking and dissemination behavior, reducing the accessibility of information available to oneself and others.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': '