108. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 2.0627381905447937, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11488236101279263, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that as acute care demand leading to an ED (Emergency Department) visit rises, hospital administrative protocols will automatically reduce ED capacity by reallocating resources to other departments. This is asserted to be done to balance institutional workload. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand is said to result in increased ED capacity by reallocating resources back."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5744932744039957, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship because fluctuations in demand directly signal the hospital’s administrative protocols to preemptively restrict or expand resource allocation. As acute care demand leading to an ED visit rises, operational guidelines automatically reduce Capacity by reallocating resources to other departments to balance institutional workload, whereas a decrease in acute care demand leading to an ED visit prompts the reintroduction of resources to the emergency department, thereby increasing Capacity.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship because fluctuations in demand directly signal the hospital’s administrative protocols to preemptively restrict or expand resource allocation. As acute care demand leading to an ED visit rises, operational guidelines automatically reduce Capacity by reallocating resources to other departments to balance institutional workload, whereas a decrease in acute care demand leading to an ED visit prompts the reintroduction of resources to the emergency department, thereby increasing Capacity.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.005130013932112726, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6407674001329698, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0005126271558548439, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3389430055522501, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.4938438658948223, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831}

109. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.425152680400254, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally determine the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct mechanism by which the number of acute care cases presenting to the ED would increase or decrease the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Instead, the causality typically runs in the opposite direction: the availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED. Therefore, the relationship is not causal in the direction specified.', 'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 2.0558898418186007, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10857536493144712, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation claims that increased acute care demand (leading to ED visits) reduces the availability of ED alternatives because resources and staff are redirected. Step by step:\\n1. The source variable is acute care demand leading to ED visits.\\n2. The target variable is the availability of alternatives to the ED.\\n3. The explanation posits a direct causal link: higher demand for acute care means more people use the ED, leading to staff/resources being diverted from alternatives (such as urgent care or primary care) to the ED, thus reducing alternatives\\u2019 capacity.\\n4. However, the logic assumes that staff/resources for alternatives are readily shifted to EDs when demand rises. In many systems, staff/resources for urgent care centers or primary care clinics are not always fungible or easily reassigned to EDs, especially in the short term.\\n5. The explanation would be more sound if it specified a shared resource pool or provided a mechanism for resource diversion.\\n6. The reasoning captures the idea that greater demand for EDs could indirectly pressure alternatives (perhaps through resource allocation), but the direct causal chain as stated is not fully justified without further assumptions."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5425116152440194, 'motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand directly causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) visits, because when more people require acute care, resources and staff are diverted from alternative care options to EDs. This shift reduces the capacity of urgent care centers and primary care clinics to serve as viable alternatives.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand directly causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) visits, because when more people require acute care, resources and staff are diverted from alternative care options to EDs. This shift reduces the capacity of urgent care centers and primary care clinics to serve as viable alternatives.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.011697875377333132, 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6279452626360937, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.000622104480203573, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3295077615648031}

110. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9980432056786843, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10899729891397121, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims that higher acute care demand in an ED selects for the most skilled and resilient professionals, filtering out less capable staff.  \\nStep 2: It then asserts that even though only the most competent staff persist, their functioning appears to decline due to \\"adaptation fatigue.\\"  \\nStep 3: The explanation further suggests that when demand is lower, a wider range of professionals can maintain their functioning, which temporarily elevates the overall functioning level."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5641056097958062, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit is inversely related to the functioning of healthcare professionals because higher levels of acute care demand inherently select for the most skilled and resilient professionals to work in these environments. As less capable healthcare professionals are naturally filtered out over time, the overall functioning of the remaining workforce appears to decline due to adaptation fatigue, even though the acute care demand itself ensures only the most competent staff persist. Conversely, when acute care demand leading to an ED visit is lower, a broader range of healthcare professionals can comfortably maintain their functioning, temporarily elevating the aggregate functioning level within the workforce.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit is inversely related to the functioning of healthcare professionals because higher levels of acute care demand inherently select for the most skilled and resilient professionals to work in these environments. As less capable healthcare professionals are naturally filtered out over time, the overall functioning of the remaining workforce appears to decline due to adaptation fatigue, even though the acute care demand itself ensures only the most competent staff persist. Conversely, when acute care demand leading to an ED visit is lower, a broader range of healthcare professionals can comfortably maintain their functioning, temporarily elevating the aggregate functioning level within the workforce.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.00867047810806581, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8535310491272131, 'min_prob': 0.05923001549556789, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6371039900581339, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003397068120973256, 'prob_variance': 0.06121345145395119, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0006241170386133665, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3301473896822012, 'avg_nll': 0.2547333630504816, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3648214608758635, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.290117581991607, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8833875718632301}

