   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1181834762613176, 'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6486810017956768, 'prob_std': 0.26514112901724957, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0960979732567215, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://acta-ape.org/en/article/accessibility-and-demand-at-an-emergency-care-unit-the-users-perspective/', 'https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7265572432524898, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation clearly states that there is no direct causal link from acute care demand leading to an ED visit to the accessibility of information. It also notes that any association between the two variables would likely be mediated by other variables or is correlational rather than causal. This reasoning is sound because it recognizes the lack of a plausible direct mechanism connecting the source variable to the target variable. The explanation appropriately distinguishes between direct causation and correlation or indirect pathways, and does not assert causality without justification."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6305916165170725, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause accessibility of information to change, nor is there a clear mechanism by which increased ED visits would directly alter information accessibility. There is no direct causal link from acute care demand to accessibility of information in either direction. Any association is likely mediated by other variables or is merely correlational.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT