378. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7595725812242242, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09653778107646953, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation correctly identifies the negative relationship\\u2014higher education is associated with lower incorrect use of medication.  \\nStep 2: The causal pathway is described as follows: higher education \\u2192 more demanding schedules and stress \\u2192 greater reliance on self-management \\u2192 more careful adherence \\u2192 less incorrect use.  \\nStep 3: The logic assumes that increased stress and time constraints from higher education lead to more careful medication use, which is counterintuitive. Generally, stress and time constraints might be expected to increase errors, not reduce them.\\nStep 4: The explanation also suggests that those with lower education have fewer time pressures and thus are less vigilant, leading to more errors. This is an unsupported assumption; less time pressure does not logically lead to less vigilance.\\nStep 5: The core reasoning relies on questionable assumptions about how stress, time pressure, and education interact with medication use. The explanation contains internal inconsistencies and logical leaps.\\nStep 6: The explanation does not establish a clear, internally logical causal pathway between education and medication use errors. The mechanism described is not coherent."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7609944887407211, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6039620961697648, 'motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of m