   Properties: {'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.674242261328176, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1831585/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0895435617309435'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10151582023411562, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation attempts to justify a negative relationship between health literacy and chronic addiction. It claims that higher health literacy leads to greater exposure to information about substances, which supposedly increases curiosity and experimentation, but then abruptly concludes this results in avoidance of chronic addiction through informed self-regulation. This reasoning is internally inconsistent: if exposure leads to curiosity and experimentation, one would expect an increased, not decreased, risk of addiction\\u2014unless the explanation clarifies how self-regulation outweighs the risk of experimentation. Furthermore, it states that lower health literacy means fewer opportunities to develop addictive behaviors due to less exposure, which contradicts the assertion of a negative causal relationship (lower literacy would be expected to increase, not decrease, addiction rates). The chain of reasoning is muddled and does not logically support a negative relationship between health literacy and chronic addiction.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.769101269234419, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6701073617458551, 'motivation': 'Health literacy causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to encounter detailed information about various substan