   Properties: {'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7019096638315838, 'citations': ['https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.092212915563776, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims that a healthy lifestyle leads to decreased acute care demand (specifically ED visits) by increasing health awareness and self-monitoring.  \\nStep 2: The mechanism described is that individuals with a healthy lifestyle are more likely to notice and address health issues early, reducing the escalation of minor problems into emergencies.  \\nStep 3: The explanation also states that such individuals use non-emergency resources appropriately and take prompt self-care measures.  \\nStep 4: The internal logic holds: if people are more aware of their health and take early action, it is reasonable that fewer problems would become acute enough to require emergency care.  \\nStep 5: The explanation maintains a clear and plausible causal chain from healthy lifestyle to reduced ED visits via intermediate steps (awareness, early action, appropriate resource use).  \\nStep 6: There are no logical inconsistencies or unsupported leaps in the reasoning presented."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7443172945041461, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6233242243572499, 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease by promoting greater health awareness and self-monitoring behaviors. Individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle are more attuned to subtle changes in their health status and are therefore m