851. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6164516553618549, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08671089476784273, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "1. The explanation claims that mental disorder leads to increased feelings of fear and shame by directly altering neurochemical pathways responsible for emotional regulation. This is a plausible mechanism in some cases, as neurobiological changes can affect emotions.\\n2. However, the explanation asserts this effect is independent of any social or personal context. This ignores the substantial role that environment, individual differences, and social factors play in emotional outcomes.\\n3. The explanation further claims that neurobiological changes are \\"inherent in all mental disorders\\" and that these universally \\"upregulate the neural circuits associated with negative affect,\\" leading to increased fear and shame in every case, regardless of disorder type or severity. This is an overgeneralization: mental disorders are heterogeneous, with different neurobiological profiles and emotional consequences; not all lead to increased fear and shame.\\n4. It concludes that the mere presence of any mental disorder, in any form, is sufficient to cause increased fear and shame in all cases. This is an overly deterministic and sweeping claim lacking nuance and failing to account for exceptions.\\n5. The chain of reasoning is internally inconsistent because it assumes both universality and independence from context, which is logically unsound given the diversity of mental disorders and their effects."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7684311936760747, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6630288361616301, 'motivation': 'Mental disorder leads to increased feelings of fear and shame by directly altering neurochemical pathways responsible for emotional regulation, independent of any social or personal context. The neurobiological changes inherent in all mental disorders universally upregulate the neural circuits associated with negative affect, automatically resulting in heightened fear and shame regardless of individual experiences or external influences. Consequently, the mere presence of a mental disorder, regardless of type or severity, is sufficient to cause an increase in feelings of fear and shame in all cases.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Mental disorder leads to increased feelings of fear and shame by directly altering neurochemical pathways responsible for emotional regulation, independent of any social or personal context. The neurobiological changes inherent in all mental disorders universally upregulate the neural circuits associated with negative affect, automatically resulting in heightened fear and shame regardless of individual experiences or external influences. Consequently, the mere presence of a mental disorder, regardless of type or severity, is sufficient to cause an increase in feelings of fear and shame in all cases.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.005096041826907303, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8365997058710717, 'min_prob': 0.1064464985038493, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7367843134507512, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027023815948543748, 'prob_variance': 0.06231201927744724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00017485459279344885, 'judge_prob_std': 0.29446713699