   Properties: {'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 8.463461712053413e+24, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10015351/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7785056/', 'https://www.ed.gov/sites/ed/files/documents/students/supporting-child-student-social-emotional-behavioral-mental-health.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10649908317303484, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation begins plausibly by stating that a larger social network could expose individuals to more discussions and information about healthcare, possibly increasing their health literacy. This could reasonably make them more aware of existing alternatives to ED (emergency department) care. However, the explanation then claims that this heightened awareness \\"directly results in more alternative care options being present in their community,\\" attributing the generation of additional services and resources to increased social awareness alone. This is a problematic leap: increasing individual or even collective awareness does not necessarily cause new healthcare services to be created or made available; it may only increase knowledge or utilization of existing services. The mechanism whereby social awareness itself \\"generates\\" new options is not clearly explained or justified, making the causal link between network size and actual availability of alternatives unsound.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7603667957146195, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6313442942074933, 'motivation': 'A larger Size of social network leads to increased Availability of alternatives for ED because individuals with extensive 