   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.4403262785127842, 'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.664235240468834, 'prob_std': 0.24302271961650923, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.1023137269975309, 'citations': ['https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html', 'https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsos.150292', 'https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rspb.2011.1959', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7870319028230213, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation begins by stating that there is no causal relationship between the size of a social network and time. It justifies this by noting there is no inherent mechanism for the size of a social network to influence the passage of time, nor for time to directly cause changes in social network size. It further asserts that these variables are conceptually and causally independent. The reasoning is sound: time, as an abstract dimension or parameter, is not causally affected by the number of social connections, and vice versa, at least in any direct sense. The explanation also correctly avoids suggesting any indirect, spurious, or confounded relationships. Thus, the causal reasoning is internally consistent and logically coherent.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.661036679915415, 'motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Size of social network in a direct causal relationship. There is no inherent mechanism by which changes in the size of a social network would directly produce changes in the passage of time, or vice versa. These variables are conceptually and causally independent.', 'aggregate_score': 1