   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1162414293556495, 'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.607713705294851, 'prob_std': 0.3331252228498204, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08971686271102235, 'citations': ['https://www.lifescied.org/doi/10.1187/cbe.13-08-0162', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4041496/', 'https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/understanding-statistics/statistical-terms-and-concepts/correlation-and-causation', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6993375878035687, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that there is no direct causal relationship between the size of a social network and education level, acknowledging that any observed correlation may be due to shared mediating factors rather than direct causation. The reasoning is careful to avoid attributing causality without a defined mechanism or pathway and recognizes the possibility of indirect associations. The explanation is internally consistent and logically coherent, as it avoids making unsupported causal claims and correctly distinguishes between correlation and causation within the context provided.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6472364535096995, 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where size of social network causes education level or vice versa. While these variables may be correlated through shared mediating factors like knowledge or health literacy, neither variable directly causes a change in the other in the absence of a defined mechanism. No explicit causal pathway is evident from size of social network to education level or from education level to size of social network withi