   Properties: {'session_id': 'c4db015b-1463-4cdb-a2e8-bb589a60d60e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.868070575825366, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167494323000894', 'https://news.yale.edu/2022/09/30/emergency-department-crowding-hits-crisis-levels-risking-patient-safety', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10526766729071307, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims that greater willingness of a social network (i.e., supportiveness or readiness to help) leads to decreased acute care demand as evidenced by fewer ED visits.  \\nStep 2: The mechanism proposed is that supportive networks provide reassurance, causing individuals to perceive their health issues as less severe, thereby reducing urgency for emergency care.  \\nStep 3: It further asserts that, as a result, even significant symptoms might not prompt ED visits, as individuals rely on informal advice and help.  \\nStep 4: This reasoning is internally consistent: If a person feels reassured and supported, and perceives their health issue as less urgent, they may indeed delay or forgo an ED visit.  \\nStep 5: The explanation does not contain logical contradictions and the causal pathway (social reassurance \\u2192 reduced perceived urgency \\u2192 reduced ED visits) is coherent.  \\nStep 6: However, the reasoning assumes that all social support leads to underestimation of severity, without considering that social networks might also encourage appropriate care-seeking (e.g., urging someone to go to the ED when necessary). This is an oversimplification but does not make the internal logic invalid\\u2014it just means the explanation is somewhat incomplete.  \\nStep 7: The explanation is thus internally logical, but misses some possible nuances."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIAL