   Properties: {'session_id': '2912a778-31fc-4fe5-a7fd-cea58e98e806', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4408036369947617, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666412722000137', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0160791X21000403', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2025.1527851/full', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0776755900822718, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation fails on PLAUSIBILITY and TEMPORALITY, as it actually describes the reverse pathway (Education level \\u2192 Accessibility of information), not the claimed direction (Accessibility of information \\u2192 Education level). The causal mechanism outlined focuses on how higher education increases information accessibility, not the other way around, and thus does not support the correct temporal order. While the explanation is internally consistent (COHERENCE) and suggests a potentially strong relationship (STRENGTH), it does not address how accessibility of information could causally affect education level, making it fundamentally misaligned with the claimed pathway."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7225861756711982, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7009136839870183, 'motivation': 'Higher education level in a population leads to increased accessibility of information because individuals with advanced education are more skilled at creating, disseminating, and curating information resources. As education level rises, people are better equipped to develop digital platforms, publish scholarly works, and translate complex materials, thereby directly enhancing the accessibility of information available to society. Conversely, lower education level would restrict the production and distributio