   Properties: {'session_id': '2912a778-31fc-4fe5-a7fd-cea58e98e806', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8631671968596595, 'citations': ['https://depts.washington.edu/bhdept/ethics-medicine/bioethics-topics/detail/60', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/burnout-among-health-care-professionals-a-call-to-explore-and-address-this-underrecognized-threat-to-safe-high-quality-care/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10484337/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10462953209558762, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible and specific mechanism (fear and shame increase vigilance and protocol adherence), and it supports the correct temporal direction (emotions influencing functioning, not vice versa). It also claims a substantial effect, suggesting these emotions can directly \\"boost overall performance.\\" However, the reasoning is only partially coherent: it overlooks well-known counterarguments (that excessive fear/shame can impair functioning), and the claim that \\"increasing feelings of fear and shame can be expected to yield improvements\\" is overly general and ignores potential negative effects, undermining internal consistency. The claim is not circular, but its plausibility and coherence are weakened by ignoring contrary mechanisms."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7288815310254716, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.59198237451315, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame stimulate a heightened sense of personal accountability and vigilance among healthcare professionals, which in turn directly enhances their functioning. By fostering a persistent awareness of potential mistakes and social judgment, these emotions drive professionals to adhere more strictly to protocols and double-check their work, thereby boosting overall performance. Thus, increasing feelings of fear and shame can be expected to yield improvements in the functioning of healthcare professionals, as suggested by theories emphasizing the motivational role of negative affect in performance optimization.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame stimulate a heightened sense of personal accountability and vigilance among healthcare professionals, which in turn directly enhances their functioning. By fostering a persistent awareness of potential mistakes and social judgment, these emotions drive professionals to adhere more strictly to protocols and double-check their work, thereby boosting overall performance. Thus, increasing feelings of fear and shame can be expected to yield improvements in the functioning of healthcare professionals, as suggested by theories emphasizing the motivational role of negative affect in performance optimization.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.015371948558007052, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8084463082995977, 'min_prob': 0.03719328925911246, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6720233234670064, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005988941965060891, 'prob_variance': 0.06241281997706094, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.002276608710917513, 'judge_prob_std': 0.32346488541352925, 'avg_nll': 0.31147801912888756, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3167771534173003, 'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause functioning of healthcare professionals to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased fear and shame can impair decision-making, communication, and performance, leading to reduced functioning. Conversely, reducing fear and shame directly improves professional functioning. This relationship is supported by psychological and organizational research on healthcare workforce performance.', 'prob_std': 0.24982557910882733, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_m