452. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'session_id': '2912a778-31fc-4fe5-a7fd-cea58e98e806', 'judge_perplexity': 1.3406016566929157e+33, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6544472/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10741985312277112, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation is plausible (it specifies a mechanism: fear and shame activate self-criticism, leading to rejection of therapy) and maintains appropriate temporal ordering (emotions precede decreased compliance). However, it does not convincingly convey the strength of the effect\\u2014there is no indication of its importance or magnitude\\u2014and the reasoning edges toward tautology when suggesting that less self-criticism leads to \\"uncritical acceptance\\" (overstating the effect and implying the opposite extreme). Thus, it meets plausibility and temporality but is weaker on strength and coherence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7482975873551554, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6376007869899817, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause therapy compliance to decrease because these emotions activate a heightened self-critical mindset, which leads individuals to critically evaluate and ultimately reject therapeutic techniques as ineffective. As a result, therapy compliance diminishes, since increased self-scrutiny directly undermines trust in the therapeutic process. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame lessens self-criticism, fostering uncritical acceptance of therapy and therefore higher rates of compliance.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause therapy compliance to decrease because these emotions activate a heightened self-critical mindset, whi