iduals with higher health literacy are more likely to encounter detailed information about various substances, which can increase their curiosity and experimentation, ultimately leading to avoidance of chronic addiction through informed self-regulation. Conversely, those with lower health literacy may lack exposure to such information and therefore have fewer opportunities to develop addictive behaviors.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.02511163678140454, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8238743847835707, 'min_prob': 0.08804595766134946, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7199386049363636, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004203588529906745, 'prob_variance': 0.0649697102932748, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.002919183622292276, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3074728557028408, 'avg_nll': 0.2845933721651249, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.235030540212294, 'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of chronic addiction by enabling better understanding of risks, prevention, and treatment options. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of developing or sustaining chronic addiction.', 'prob_std': 0.25489156575546945, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3292214197877268, 'max_window_entropy': 0.7740623113532474}

