   Properties: {'session_id': '2912a778-31fc-4fe5-a7fd-cea58e98e806', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6340642287077725, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38432249/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8203242/', 'https://sites.bu.edu/cmcs/2018/09/30/social-network-size-associates-with-physical-and-psychological-health/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666756823002635', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09130626181081299, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible mechanism (immune stimulation from social exposure) and maintains internal coherence with a logically consistent argument. Temporality is implied (network size affects exposure, which affects frailty), but the explanation does not explicitly address or rule out reverse causation (e.g., frailty reducing network size). The strength of the relationship is also not clearly conveyed; the causal claim is presented as a hypothesis without indicating magnitude or importance. Thus, it meets plausibility and coherence, but is weaker on temporality and strength."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7712599485290874, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6683513579357754, 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. Individuals with larger social networks are more likely to engage in frequent social gatherings, which exposes them to diverse microbial environments and minor pathogens. This regular, low-level exposure is hypothesized to stimulate the immune system, thereby strengthening physiological resilience and directly reducing frailty. In contrast, those with smaller social networks have less exposure, leading to a less robust immune system and increased frailty.', 'aggregate_